          "The work has ninety-five parts, and the number never varies: no manuscript tradition or edition divides it differently. In the manuscripts and in the critical edition, each part is headed ԲԱՆ with a number. Ban, plural bank, is an ordinary Armenian word meaning word, speech or discourse. It is also the word used to translate the Greek logos at the opening of John's Gospel. So the parts are not chapters in the usual sense. They are speeches, each one an address that is begun and completed.",
          "Translators have rendered ban in almost every possible way, which is why English descriptions of the book disagree. Terian, Samuelian and the Matenadaran call them prayers. Jesse Arlen calls them discourses. The French tradition and most encyclopedias call them chapters, and Kudian's title calls them soliloquies. Calling them ninety-five poems is not wrong but loses something, because it suggests free-standing lyrics instead of parts of one long address. There is no single way of citing them either: Terian numbers by prayer and section, Arlen by discourse and lettered subsection, Samuelian by number and letter.",
          "The order is deliberate. Mahe proposed reading the ninety-five as a journey through an Armenian church, from the narthex where penitents stood, to the nave, to the sanctuary. Narekatsi himself describes the book as built like an edifice. A few individual pieces have been questioned. Mkrtich Mkryan argued on grounds of style that discourse 93 and parts of 26, 33 and 34 read like theological prose, unlike the rest, and might not be the author's. The 1985 critical edition examined the manuscripts and kept them, and the doubt now survives only as a footnote in the scholarship.",
