          "The language is grabar, Classical Armenian, which by the tenth century was a learned written language that nobody spoke. Narekatsi pushes it to its limits: long chains of near-synonyms, compounds coined on the spot, and sentences stretched far beyond the length the language was built to carry. The difficulty is not just a modern reader's problem. Medieval Armenian readers found the book hard too, and a large tradition of commentary exists precisely because the text needed explaining almost from the start.",
          "Everyone mentions his word-building, and the figures attached to it need care. The Matenadaran, citing the linguist Vardan Arakelyan, puts the number of new words in the Book of Lamentations at about seven hundred. The often-repeated figure of more than two and a half thousand coinages comes from Hrachik Mirzoyan and covers all of Narekatsi's writing, not this book alone. The two numbers do not compete, and quoting either one as the count for this work would misrepresent it.",
          "Nor did he invent from nothing. His teacher Anania of Narek had written on compunction and tears, and specialists see the Book of Lamentations as developing that teaching, not breaking away from it. Behind both stands a longer ascetic tradition: Ephrem the Syrian, Evagrius, John Cassian, John Climacus, Isaac of Nineveh, and Narekatsi's own contemporary Symeon the New Theologian. Narekatsi did not invent the penitential prayer. He took an inherited form to a scale and a linguistic intensity that Armenian had never seen before.",
