          "The Psalms are the model, and the debt runs deep into the book's structure. The Psalter supplies a grammar of address: a first person that can accuse itself before God without collapsing, and can speak for a whole community while still saying I. That is exactly what the Book of Lamentations does at length, and several discourses meditate directly on individual psalms.",
          "The references to scripture are so dense that the meaning depends on them. When modern Armenian translations left the biblical allusions without notes, whole passages became unclear, which shows that the references carry meaning and are not just decoration. The medieval commentators understood this. When Nerses of Lambron discussed the thirty-third discourse in 1177, his method was to set Narekatsi's prayer beside the biblical books it draws on.",
          "One distinction here is often blurred. Prayers and hymns by Narekatsi did enter Armenian church services, and some of his chants are still sung. The Book of Lamentations itself, though, is a private prayer book, not a liturgical one. It is not a fixed text in the Divine Liturgy or the Book of Hours. Its life has been devotional: read alone, read aloud beside the sick, kept in the house.",
