          "Bakunts came to fiction late, and by an unusual route. He had trained as an agriculturalist, and from 1924 to 1926 he was chief agronomist of Zangezur, the official responsible for the land and crops of the region where these stories are set. He was no visitor taking notes. He had already surveyed these same fields on paper.",
          "He chose the short story and treated it as a form in its own right, not as a small novel. Armine Manasyan says his plots do not develop an action through stages. Instead, they pile up a character's boundary situations until they reach one final limit-collision. Aghababyan says the same thing from the other side: these stories have no special beginnings and no special endings.",
          "He also stayed close to fact. Aghababyan argues that Bakunts followed the principle that the subject is the object. He borrowed rather than invented, taking real events, real faces, things he had watched and things he had been told. Sometimes he even added a note, like a medieval scribe's colophon, to record that an episode had really happened. The title story is the case we know most about, and it has its own section below.",
          "Critics have argued over where his prose came from. Aghababyan traced his realist method to the prose of Hovhannes Tumanyan. Others have heard Terian in it instead, and a symbolist tone. One attempt to cut the Tumanyan link altogether rested on counting epithets: Tumanyan, it argued, reflects the world directly, while Bakunts gives a mediated, subjective image of it. Manasyan, who reports the debate, finds that counting mechanical and its conclusion exaggerated.",
