          "In Mtnadzor, landscape is not a backdrop for the action. Manasyan puts it most sharply: nature here is not described but brought into the story by the teller as a minimum unit of psychological movement. A ridge, a change in the weather or a bird does the work another writer would give to a sentence about what a character felt.",
          "Aghababyan describes the book's world as one where reality and legend have not yet separated. Man and bear live side by side, and there is no real line between the village and the country around it: nature is inside the village, and the village lies in nature's lap. His proof comes from the book's own economics. Leave the fields at Akar unploughed for two years, and the forest takes them back.",
          "Naira Hambardzumyan reads the same landscape as holding time rather than space. She starts from the book's own sentence, that Mtnadzor seems a forgotten corner from the days when there was not yet any man. The mythic time this opens up, she argues, moves slowly towards the present until the two occupy each other. That is why the forest in these stories can feel older than the people in it, without any claim of the supernatural.",
