          "The most common misreading of this book takes it for a fond picture of village life, and it was misread that way in its own decade. Bakunts had already rejected that reading in his journalism before the book appeared. Aghababyan quotes him attacking the myth of the pastoral village head-on: the dark and ignorant peasant does not come by the dry bread he eats so easily, and there is no idyll at all of the kind the poet sings about.",
          "The stories bear this out in detail. They are full of debt, interest and the arithmetic of survival. Aghababyan's example is a peasant who keeps his accounts in an old dream-book. He enters milk yields and mill queues, and then his own sister's burial as a cost, so absorbed in the details of his everyday tragedy, as Aghababyan puts it, that he has no time left to live through it. Elsewhere there is superstition, a headman who burns a man's house and has him deported, a bride examined by her suitor's family, a whip across a villager's shoulders.",
          "Aghababyan's conclusion, which this article follows, is that however remote and forgotten Mtnadzor is, the same laws operate there as in what he calls the centres of enlightenment. Eddie Arnavoudian, reviewing the English translation, reaches the same point on his own. There is nothing idyllic about the Dark Valley, he writes: it is dark not through any romanticised isolation, but because it is impoverished and outside the mainstream of urban life. He reads Bakunts as placing human misery in socially constructed relations, not in inescapable flaws of human nature.",
          "None of this makes the book a tract. Victoria Rowe, introducing the English edition, is careful on this point. The portraits are sympathetic as well as stark, and they are not propaganda pieces, something, she notes, that the Soviet authorities understood perfectly well. The beauty of the country and the hardness of living in it are both fully present. The tension between them is the book's subject, not a flaw in it.",
