      "Baghdasar Aghbar is a comedy in three acts by Hakob Paronyan, the satirist of Ottoman Constantinople, written in 1886. It is about a man who is entirely in the right and is ruined by it. Baghdasar has seen his wife leaving a house with another man. He wants a divorce, and takes the case to the tribunal his community keeps for such matters. Over three acts, the lawyer he hires, the friend who is his wife's lover and the councillors who are supposed to judge the case agree among themselves that he saw nothing. By the end he is repeating their version in front of witnesses. Paronyan died in 1891 without seeing this or any of his other plays on a stage.",
