          "The notes to the Armenian collected works list the early translations, and they form a useful sequence. A French version appeared at Paris in 1913 as Maître Balthasar, a comedy in three acts. An English one was published at Boston in 1933 as Uncle Balthazar, translated by Ervant D. Megerditchian. The editors note, with visible irritation, that he renamed four of the characters for no reason they could discover, the three councillors among them. A Russian translation by Ya. Khachatryants appeared at Moscow in 1937 and was later reissued.",
          "Two modern English versions exist, and between them they define the title problem described above: Kudian's Balthazar of 1992, which drops the Armenian word, and the 2020 Baghdasar Akhpar, which keeps it and glosses it. Anyone looking for the play in English should be ready to find it under any of these names.",
