      "Land of Nairi is a novel in three parts by Yeghishe Charents, the modernist poet of early Soviet Armenia. He wrote it between 1921 and 1925, and it was published at Yerevan in 1926. Its subject is a provincial Armenian city closely modeled on Kars, the fortress city where Charents was born. The novel follows the city from patriotic confidence through the First World War to a collapse that leaves its grandest ambitions in ruins. Near the start the narrator announces that the novel has no hero. The rest of the book tests an old, lofty idea of the homeland, Nairi, against a real historical catastrophe, without simply celebrating it or rejecting it.",
